hping3 -- building, testing and installing
==========================================

Requirements
------------

  - a C compiler (GCC or Clang; C99 or later)
  - GNU make (called "gmake" on the BSDs)
  - libpcap with its headers (libpcap-dev / libpcap-devel)
  - optional: Tcl 8.6 with its headers (tcl-dev / tcl-devel) for the
    scripting mode. Without Tcl, hping3 builds as a command line tool only.
  - root privileges are needed to *run* hping3 (raw sockets, packet
    capture), never to build or test it.

Supported/tested platforms
--------------------------

  Linux (x86_64, glibc) is the platform the build and the test-suite are
  verified on. The BSD/Solaris/macOS code paths are still present but were
  not built as part of the current work; treat them as "compiles maybe,
  untested" until they are added to the CI matrix.

Quick start
-----------

  $ ./configure            # or: ./configure --no-tcl / --with-tcl
  $ make
  $ make check             # offline test-suite, no root, no traffic
  $ sudo make install

configure options and variables
-------------------------------

  --prefix=DIR      installation prefix           (default /usr/local)
  --sbindir=DIR     where hping3 goes             (default PREFIX/sbin)
  --mandir=DIR      manual pages                  (default PREFIX/share/man)
  --datadir=DIR     Tcl example scripts           (default PREFIX/share)
  --no-tcl          build without Tcl scripting
  --with-tcl        require Tcl: configure fails if it is not usable
                    (the default, without either option, is to use Tcl
                    when a working installation is found)

  Environment: CC CPPFLAGS CFLAGS LDFLAGS LDLIBS AR RANLIB PKG_CONFIG
               TCLSH TCL_CONFIG (path to tclConfig.sh)
               HPING_OSTYPE (override "uname -s" for cross builds)

  Tcl is looked for, in this order, through TCL_CONFIG, TCLSH,
  pkg-config (tcl, tcl9.0, tcl8.6, tcl8.5), the usual tclConfig.sh
  locations and finally a tclsh in PATH. A found Tcl is only used if a
  test program builds and links against it. Tcl 8.6 is the version the
  test-suite runs with; Tcl 9 detection exists but is not verified yet.

  libpcap is located through pkg-config or pcap-config, falling back to
  plain -lpcap. configure exits with status 1 if pcap.h/libpcap, the
  compiler, or an explicitly required Tcl are missing; the failing test
  program output is left in .configure-tmp/t.log.

  Byte order is taken from the compiler's __BYTE_ORDER__ macro, so
  cross-compiling works with any GCC/Clang; only compilers without that
  macro fall back to running the byteorder.c probe.

make targets
------------

  make                 build ./hping3
  make check           build and run the test-suite (tests/)
  make WERROR=1        treat compiler warnings as errors (CI does this)
  make SANITIZE=1 ...  build with ASan/UBSan; combine with check, e.g.
                       make clean && make check SANITIZE=1
  make install         install into DESTDIR/prefix (see below)
  make uninstall       remove what install created
  make libars.a        the ARS packet library as a static archive
  make clean / distclean

Installation layout and staging
-------------------------------

  make install DESTDIR=/tmp/stage   # nothing is written outside DESTDIR

  sbindir/hping3            the binary (mode 755)
  sbindir/hping, hping2     relative symlinks to hping3 (ln -sf: repeated
                            installs just replace them)
  mandir/man8/hping3.8      manual page
  datadir/hping3/lib/*.htcl Tcl example scripts (only with Tcl support)

  Paths containing spaces are supported (they are quoted in the recipes).
  Nothing is made setuid: run hping3 as root or with the needed
  capabilities. See the README about the risks of setuid installations.

Test-suite
----------

  "make check" builds tests/test_core, tests/test_waitpacket,
  tests/test_scan, tests/test_loop, tests/test_parse and (with Tcl)
  tests/test_script, runs them, then runs tests/cli.sh (--help/--version/
  option validation against ./hping3), tests/install.sh (staged install
  into a temporary directory) and tests/libars.sh (a program linked
  against libars.a alone). All of it runs as an ordinary user without
  opening a socket: packets are crafted in memory or read from pcap
  savefiles written by the tests themselves, and the event loop is driven
  by a fake clock.

  The historical scripts in lib/regtest send real packets and are not
  part of "make check".

Historical notes
----------------

  Earlier versions required copying a BSD net/bpf.h on some systems; the
  header is now only included where it exists (configure checks for it).
  The "make install" of previous versions wrote absolute symlinks into
  /usr/sbin; that is what DESTDIR/prefix replace.
